 "note": "Two 1891 households from the Lenton Road page, transcribed by A. Hagues: schedule 181, 8 Lenton Road (#182), and schedule 182, 6 Lenton Road (#179). Neither house held an 1891 census record.\n\nThomas Moore, commercial traveller, born in Ireland, with his wife, son and two servants, is new to the record. Wright's Directory of Nottingham has Miss Ellen M. Cowan at 8 Lenton Road by 1894-95, so the Moores had moved on by then.\n\nWilliam Lewis, cork cutter, his wife Elizabeth, six grown children and two servants. William (#813), Ellen Frances (#814) and Elizabeth Beatrice (#816) were already in the record from 1911 at 6 Lenton Road and are bound by number, so they gain 1891 rather than being made again; the rest are new. The directory prints William Lewis, cork cutter, at 6 Lenton Road in 1894-95, 1898-99 and 1910-11. Ages are as the enumerator wrote them, so a birth year worked back from one may be a year out either way.\n\n**#813 became #3794.** The head of 6 Lenton Road, 59 in 1891, cork cutter, born Nottingham about 1832. The record carries him as **William W Lewis (#3794)**.\n\n**But #3794 is itself two men merged into one record** - this cork cutter of 6 Lenton Road, born Nottingham, and a hosiery manufacturer of 3 Pelham Crescent, born Tewkesbury, who are heads of two houses on the same night in 1891. When that record is split, this binding must follow the Lenton Road man.",
 "source": "1891 census, 8 and 6 Lenton Road, schedules 181 and 182, transcribed by A. Hagues. Directory evidence: Wright's Directory of Nottingham 1894-95, page 84 (Leicester Special Collections id 164590); 1898-99, page 92 (id 165268); 1910-11, page 123 (id 165799).",
